\cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 Literally:\'a0
\f4\i the brother-and-sister who became the sun and the moon.
\f2\i0 \'a0Everything before 
\f5 \'bf\'c0\'b4\'a9\'c0\'cc
\f2  is one relative clause modifying it, which is why Korean puts the transformation first and the people last.\cb1 \
\cb3 The one word worth pausing on is\'a0
\f6 \'bf\'c0\'b4\'a9\'c0\'cc
\f2 . It isn't generic "siblings." It's built from 
\f5 \'bf\'c0\'b6\'f3\'ba\'f1
\f2  (a woman's older brother) plus 
\f5 \'b4\'a9\'c0\'cc
\f2  (a man's sister), and it specifically means\'a0
\f3 a brother-and-sister pair
\f2 , opposite sexes, defined by their relation to each other. Korean has separate words for two brothers (
\f5 \'c7\'fc\'c1\'a6
\f2 ) and two sisters (
\f5 \'c0\'da\'b8\'c5
\f2 ). So the title doesn't say "two children became the sun and moon," it says\'a0
\f3 a matched pair, male and female, became the two lights.
\f2 \cb1 \
\cb3 Also note 
\f5 \'b4\'de\'c0\'cc
\f2  carries the subject marker, which is exactly why it's pronounced\'a0
\f4\i dari
\f2\i0 \'a0and why my Whisper runs kept producing 
\f5 \'b4\'d9\'b8\'ae
\f2 . That liaison is what made the folktale reading phonetically plausible in the first place.\cb1 \
